BEGIN:VEVENT
DTSTAMP:20260621T234500Z
UID:event-assignment-507037
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE;VALUE=DATE:20260823
CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:---------------------------------------------------------------
 -------------------------\nProfessional Development through PERMA-V and SC
 ARF Lenses and Decision-Making Frameworks\n-------------------------------
 ---------------------------------------------------------\n\nOverview\nTyp
 e: Individual assignment\nWeight: 30% of total course grade\nTotal Word li
 mit: 2\,500 words (±10%) \n   For tasks 1\,2\, and 3        1000 w
 ords  (±10%) \n    For task 4                        1500
  words (±10%) \n\nIf anything about assignment 1 is unclear\, post your 
 question on the [discussion board.] (https://canvas.auckland.ac.nz/courses
 /145185/discussion_topics/1637606) You can connect with fellow students th
 ere\, and a graduate teaching assistant will respond to any unanswered que
 ries.\nFor questions that can’t be resolved on the board or any other is
 sues\, contact Akhilnandh Ramesh directly at [aram621@aucklanduni.ac.nz] (
 mailto:aram621@aucklanduni.ac.nz).\n\n \n\nAssignment Brief\n\nThis is an
  individual assignment worth 30% of the total course grade. It is designed
  to expose postgraduate students to leadership decision-making in real-wor
 ld professional contexts\, where judgment\, accountability\, and ethical a
 wareness are required.\n\nStudents are required to individually describe a
 nd critically examine a key workplace decision-making scenario involving p
 lanning\, analysis\, and prioritisation. The scenario should reflect a com
 plex professional decision that required the exercise of leadership judgem
 ent rather than routine or procedural action.\n\nThe assignment focuses on
  how decisions are shaped through human reasoning\, reflection\, and accou
 ntability. Where AI-led decision-making tools or analytical systems are us
 ed\, students are expected to examine how these tools support the decision
 -making process while ensuring that responsibility for the final decision 
 remains with the individual.\n\nStudents must analyse the decision-making 
 process using the PERMA-V and SCARF frameworks\, demonstrating reflective 
 and critical thinking rather than surface-level description. The analysis 
 should consider how well-being\, motivation\, social dynamics\, autonomy\,
  fairness\, and ethical responsibility influenced both the decision proces
 s and its outcome.\n\nGiven the increasing use of AI-led decision-making t
 ools in professional contexts\, students are expected to demonstrate aware
 ness of their limitations and risks\, including potential bias\, unintende
 d favouritism\, and over-reliance on automated recommendations. The emphas
 is of this assignment remains on leadership judgement and ethical accounta
 bility\, with AI-led tools treated strictly as decision-support mechanisms
  rather than decision-makers.\n\nAI use policy\n\n* The use of Artificial 
 Intelligence (AI) tools is permitted and expected in this assignment.\n\n*
  AI must be used responsibly\, ethically\, and transparently. students are
  required to apply professional judgement and ethical awareness when using
  AI.\n\n* Students remain fully accountable for all decisions\, interpreta
 tions\, and conclusions presented\, regardless of AI involvement.\n\n* AI 
 outputs must not be accepted uncritically.\n\n* Students are expected to a
 pply professional judgement when using AI-supported insights.\n\n* AI-gene
 rated outputs should be validated\, challenged\, and contextualised using 
 domain knowledge and real-world understanding.\n\n* Students must reflect 
 on limitations\, assumptions\, biases\, or favouritism that may be introdu
 ced through AI use.\n\n* The focus of assessment is on leadership-oriented
  decision-making and reflection\, not on AI-generated content or automated
  answers.\n\n* The student needs to maintain an AI Evidence Log\, document
 ing representative prompts\, iterations\, rejected outputs and lessons lea
 rned.\n\nThe analysis must be grounded in the PERMA-V and SCARF frameworks
  and demonstrate reflective and critical thinking rather than surface-leve
 l description.\n\nOverall total word limit: 2\,500 words ±10%.\n\nTask 4 
 should be approximately 1500 words ±10%. words. For the remaining tasks\,
  Students are encouraged to allocate the overall word limit reasonably acr
 oss the tasks\, ensuring each task is addressed with sufficient depth and 
 reflection. Task-wise word limits are not prescribed and are deliberately 
 left open-ended. As this is a leadership-focused course\, students are exp
 ected to exercise their own professional judgement in determining how best
  to distribute emphasis across the tasks\, ensuring a clear linkage betwee
 n them and a clear flow of ideas and context.\n\n \n\nAssignment Tasks\n\
 nTask 1: Decision Context and Objective (10%)\n\nIn this task\, you are re
 quired to reflect on your own leadership-oriented decision-making in a com
 plex professional context. The purpose of this task is to develop your abi
 lity to exercise judgement\, take accountability\, and demonstrate ethical
  awareness\, with AI serving as a supporting aid rather than the driver of
  decisions.\n\nYou must describe a significant decision-making scenario fr
 om your professional experience (e.g.\, planning\, analysis\, prioritisati
 on\, or evaluation) and provide a reflective account of how you approached
 \, executed\, and evaluated the decision. Your focus should be on how you 
 thought\, reasoned\, and exercised leadership\, not on demonstrating techn
 ical AI capability.\n\nIn your response\, you should:\n\n* Describe the de
 cision context\, explaining why the decision was complex and required lead
 ership judgement.\n\n* Reflect on how you approached the decision\, includ
 ing how you framed the problem\, evaluated options\, and managed trade-off
 s.\n\n* Critically evaluate how the decision was ultimately made\, includi
 ng what worked well and what you would do differently in hindsight.\n\nIf 
 you used AI tools\, you must justify why AI support was appropriate in com
 parison to relying solely on your own judgement. Routine data processing o
 r basic numerical analysis alone is not considered meaningful AI use. Whil
 e AI may assist with summarising information or handling large volumes of 
 data\, this task focuses on the use of AI to support insight generation\, 
 structured reasoning\, and synthesis\, with final accountability remaining
  with you as the decision-maker.\n\nYour decision-making scenario must cle
 arly address the following elements:\n\n* Your problem or opportunity stat
 ement\n\n* Your decision objective(s) and success criteria\n\n* The scope 
 and boundaries of your decision\n\n* Your assumptions\, constraints\, and 
 key risks\n\n* Your leadership considerations around AI use\, including wh
 ere AI added value and where you deliberately chose not to use it\n\n* The
  ethical\, professional\, or organisational concerns you were mindful of t
 hroughout the decision-making process\n\nExample of Decision-making contex
 ts:\n\n* Engineering or operations planning\n\n* Supply Chain\, Inventory 
 or resource allocation decisions\n\n* Project management\, task prioritisa
 tion or risk assessment\n\n* Performance feedback or reflections on the te
 am’s performance\n\n* Strategic decision-making in an organization withi
 n your work.\n\n* Students with limited professional work experience may u
 se relevant academic experiences\, including research projects\, internshi
 ps\, or major assignments completed as part of their studies.\n\n \n\nTas
 k 2: PERMA-V Reflection on Decision-Making (15%)\n\nIn this task\, you are
  required to reflect on your decision-making experience through the PERMA-
 V framework\, with primary emphasis on leadership behaviour\, wellbeing\, 
 and professional judgement. The ethical and responsible use of AI-based de
 cision-support tools is permitted\, but your reflection must remain centre
 d on human experience and accountability.\n\nYou should critically examine
  how the decision-making process\, including any use of analytical or AI-s
 upported insights\, influenced your own experience as a decision-maker and
  the experience of others involved.\n\nIn your reflection\, address the fo
 llowing dimensions:\n\n* Positive Emotion\nHow did the decision-making pro
 cess affect your emotional state (e.g.\, confidence\, stress\, reassurance
 \, uncertainty)? Did the availability of AI-supported insights influence e
 motional responses\, either positively or negatively?\n\n* Engagement\nTo 
 what extent were you cognitively and emotionally engaged in the decision? 
 Did AI-supported analysis enhance focus and sense-making\, or did it risk 
 over-reliance or reduced critical engagement?\n\n* Relationships\nHow did 
 the decision process affect relationships with colleagues or stakeholders?
  Consider whether AI-supported inputs influenced trust\, credibility\, or 
 communication dynamics.\n\n* Meaning\nHow did the decision align with your
  personal values and leadership identity? Reflect on whether the use of AI
 -supported insights supported or challenged your sense of purpose and resp
 onsibility.\n\n* Accomplishment\nHow do you evaluate the outcome of the de
 cision in terms of effectiveness and learning? Did AI-supported reasoning 
 contribute meaningfully to perceived success?\n\n* Vitality\nReflect on th
 e mental and emotional energy required. Did AI-supported assistance reduce
  cognitive load\, or introduce additional effort through validation and ov
 ersight?\n\nYour response should demonstrate reflective depth\, showing ho
 w leadership decisions (maybe supported by but not driven by AI)\, influen
 ce wellbeing and performance.\n\nTask 3: SCARF-Based Social and Leadership
  Analysis (15%)\n\nIn this task\, you are required to analyse the social a
 nd leadership dynamics of your decision-making scenario using the SCARF fr
 amework. Ethical and transparent use of AI-based decision-support tools is
  permitted\, and you should reflect on how their use influenced social per
 ceptions and interactions.\n\nYou should examine how the decision process\
 , including the presence of AI-supported recommendations or analyses\, aff
 ected the experience of those involved.\n\nAddress the following dimension
 s:\n\n* Status\nHow did the decision process affect perceptions of compete
 nce\, authority\, or expertise (yours and others’)? Did the use of AI-su
 pported insights enhance or undermine professional status?\n\n* Certainty\
 nDid the decision process increase clarity and predictability\, or introdu
 ce ambiguity? How did AI-supported analysis influence your ability to mana
 ge uncertainty?\n\n* Autonomy\nHow much choice and control did you and oth
 ers retain? Did AI-supported recommendations support informed autonomy\, o
 r constrain independent judgement?\n\n* Relatedness\nHow did the decision 
 process affect trust\, psychological safety\, and connection among stakeho
 lders? Consider whether AI involvement influenced openness or scepticism.\
 n\n* Fairness\nWere decisions perceived as fair and transparent? Reflect o
 n whether AI-supported inputs raised or mitigated concerns around bias or 
 favouritism.\n\nYour analysis should demonstrate insight into ethical lead
 ership and social behaviour\, reinforcing that AI-supported tools can infl
 uence human dynamics and must be used with care and accountability.\n\nTas
 k 4: Development and Application of a Personal Ethical Decision-Making Fra
 mework (60%)\n\nReflect on the leadership concepts\, policies\, and decisi
 on-making approaches introduced throughout the course and create a persona
 l ethical decision-making framework. The purpose of this task is to demons
 trate leadership maturity by moving beyond the application of existing mod
 els and developing a context-sensitive\, strengths-based framework that su
 pports ethical and accountable judgment in complex professional situations
 .\n\nThe proposed framework should be original\, clearly structured\, and 
 grounded in the course's learning objectives. It can focus on strengths pr
 ofile assessment\, including how leaders can capitalise on strengths while
  identifying and defending against weaknesses in themselves and others.\n\
 nThe framework should incorporate multiple layers of influence on decision
 -making\, including (but not limited to):\n\n* A pyramid of influence cove
 ring knowledge\, skills\, behaviours\, beliefs\, values\, self-image\, ide
 ntity\, and purpose\n\n* The role of cognitive biases\, identity-based mot
 ivators\, personal characteristics\, and personality traits\n\n* The impac
 t of lifestyle and contextual pressures on productivity\, judgement\, and 
 behaviour\n\n* The interaction between knowledge\, behaviour\, and perform
 ance outcomes\n\n* The influence of career and value anchors (e.g. Schein
 ’s anchors) on priorities\, ethical sensitivity\, and leadership judgeme
 nt\n\n* The developed framework should be applied to the decision context 
 described in task 1\, but before you apply\, you’d need to classify the 
 decision context described in Task 1 before applying the framework. Clearl
 y identify:\n\n* The sector in which the decision occurs (e.g. industry\, 
 academia\, research\, public sector\, healthcare\, education)\n\n* The nat
 ure of sector-specific decision-making involved (e.g. operational\, strate
 gic\, ethical\, people-centred\, risk-based)\n\nUse this classification to
  justify how ethical considerations\, accountability expectations\, and le
 adership responsibilities are framed within the scenario.\n\n* Apply the p
 roposed decision-making framework to the scenario and critically discuss t
 he insights and outcomes it generates. The discussion should address the f
 ollowing:\n\n* Professional judgement and accountability:\nHow responsibil
 ity for decisions is retained when using decision-support tools (including
  analytical or AI-assisted tools)\, and how the framework prevents over-re
 liance on such tools.\n\n* Impact on people:\nHow the framework considers 
 the effects of decisions on motivation\, trust\, autonomy\, psychological 
 safety\, and perceptions of fairness.\n\n* Balancing performance\, wellbei
 ng\, and ethics:\nHow the framework supports ethical responsibility and we
 llbeing while still achieving performance outcomes in complex or uncertain
  situations.\n\n* Is applicable to similar decisions within the same conte
 xt or sector\n\n* Can be embedded into organisational or institutional pra
 ctice\n\n* Could function as a shared decision-making guide or “ready-re
 ckoner” for others in similar roles (e.g. colleagues\, peers\, or fellow
  students)\n\nThis evaluation should reflect how individuals formalise dec
 ision-making practices that guide leadership judgement while remaining gro
 unded in organisational values\, ethical principles\, and collective wisdo
 m.\n\nYour response should demonstrate thoughtful synthesis and leadership
  maturity\, showing how insights from the course help you construct a deci
 sion making model that meaningfully informs real-world leadership behaviou
 r.
