 e_145185&month=08&year=2026#assignment_507037
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://instructu
 re-uploads-apse2.s3.ap-southeast-2.amazonaws.com/account_61990000000000001
 /attachments/18034557/prod-uoa-canvas-mobile.css"><link rel="stylesheet" h
 ref="https://instructure-uploads-apse2.s3.ap-southeast-2.amazonaws.com/acc
 ount_61990000000000001/attachments/3526955/uoa-mobile.css"><h2><span style
 ="font-size: 18pt\;"><strong>Professional Development through PERMA-V and 
 SCARF Lenses and Decision-Making Frameworks</strong></span></h2>\n<p><span
  style="text-decoration: underline\;"><span style="font-size: 14pt\;">Over
 view</span></span><br><span style="font-size: 14pt\;">Type: Individual ass
 ignment</span><br><span style="font-size: 14pt\;">Weight: 30% of total cou
 rse grade</span><br><span style="font-size: 14pt\;">Total Word limit: 2\,5
 00 words (±10%)&nbsp\;</span><br><span style="font-size: 14pt\;">&nbsp\; 
 <span style="font-size: 12pt\;">&nbsp\;For tasks 1\,2\, and 3&nbsp\; &nbsp
 \; &nbsp\; &nbsp\; 1000 words &nbsp\;(±10%)&nbsp\;</span></span><br><span
  style="font-size: 12pt\;">&nbsp\; &nbsp\; For task 4&nbsp\; &nbsp\; &nbsp
 \; &nbsp\; &nbsp\; &nbsp\; &nbsp\; &nbsp\; &nbsp\; &nbsp\; &nbsp\; &nbsp\;
  1500 words (±10%)&nbsp\;</span></p>\n<p>If anything about assignment 1 i
 s unclear\, post your question on the <a title="Assignment 1 Q&amp\;A" hre
 f="https://canvas.auckland.ac.nz/courses/145185/discussion_topics/1637606"
  data-course-type="discussions" data-published="true" data-api-endpoint="h
 ttps://canvas.auckland.ac.nz/api/v1/courses/145185/discussion_topics/16376
 06" data-api-returntype="Discussion"><strong>discussion board</strong>.</a
 > You can connect with fellow students there\, and a graduate teaching ass
 istant will respond to any unanswered queries.<br>For questions that can
 ’t be resolved on the board or any other issues\, contact Akhilnandh Ram
 esh directly at <a href="mailto:aram621@aucklanduni.ac.nz" target="_blank"
 ><strong>aram621@aucklanduni.ac.nz</strong></a>.</p>\n<p>&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><
 strong>Assignment Brief</strong></p>\n<p><span>This is an individual assig
 nment worth 30% of the total course grade. It is designed to expose postgr
 aduate students to leadership decision-making in real-world professional c
 ontexts\, where judgment\, accountability\, and ethical awareness are requ
 ired.</span></p>\n<p><span>Students are required to individually describe 
 and critically examine a key workplace decision-making scenario involving 
 planning\, analysis\, and prioritisation. The scenario should reflect a co
 mplex professional decision that required the exercise of leadership judge
 ment rather than routine or procedural action.</span></p>\n<p><span>The as
 signment focuses on how decisions are shaped through human reasoning\, ref
 lection\, and accountability. Where AI-led decision-making tools or analyt
 ical systems are used\, students are expected to examine how these tools s
 upport the decision-making process while ensuring that responsibility for 
 the final decision remains with the individual.</span></p>\n<p><span>Stude
 nts must analyse the decision-making process using the PERMA-V and SCARF f
 rameworks\, demonstrating reflective and critical thinking rather than sur
 face-level description. The analysis should consider how well-being\, moti
 vation\, social dynamics\, autonomy\, fairness\, and ethical responsibilit
 y influenced both the decision process and its outcome.</span></p>\n<p><sp
 an>Given the increasing use of AI-led decision-making tools in professiona
 l contexts\, students are expected to demonstrate awareness of their limit
 ations and risks\, including potential bias\, unintended favouritism\, and
  over-reliance on automated recommendations. The emphasis of this assignme
 nt remains on leadership judgement and ethical accountability\, with AI-le
 d tools treated strictly as decision-support mechanisms rather than decisi
 on-makers.</span></p>\n<p><strong>AI use policy</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Th
 e use of Artificial Intelligence (AI) tools is permitted and expected in t
 his assignment.</li>\n<li>AI must be used responsibly\, ethically\, and tr
 ansparently. students are required to apply professional judgement and eth
 ical awareness when using AI.</li>\n<li>Students remain fully accountable 
 for all decisions\, interpretations\, and conclusions presented\, regardle
 ss of AI involvement.</li>\n<li>AI outputs must not be accepted uncritical
 ly.</li>\n<li>Students are expected to apply professional judgement when u
 sing AI-supported insights.</li>\n<li>AI-generated outputs should be valid
 ated\, challenged\, and contextualised using domain knowledge and real-wor
 ld understanding.</li>\n<li>Students must reflect on limitations\, assumpt
 ions\, biases\, or favouritism that may be introduced through AI use.</li>
 \n<li>The focus of assessment is on leadership-oriented decision-making an
 d reflection\, not on AI-generated content or automated answers.</li>\n<li
 >The student needs to maintain an AI Evidence Log\, documenting representa
 tive prompts\, iterations\, rejected outputs and lessons learned.</li>\n</
 ul>\n<p>The analysis must be grounded in the <strong>PERMA-V</strong> and 
 <strong>SCARF</strong> frameworks and demonstrate reflective and critical 
 thinking rather than surface-level description.</p>\n<p>Overall total word
  limit: <strong>2\,500 words ±10%</strong><span>.</span></p>\n<p>Task 4 s
 hould be approximately <strong>1500 words ±10%</strong><span>.<strong> </
 strong></span>words. For the remaining tasks\, Students are encouraged to 
 allocate the overall word limit reasonably across the tasks\, ensuring eac
 h task is addressed with sufficient depth and reflection. Task-wise word l
 imits are not prescribed and are deliberately left open-ended. As this is 
 a leadership-focused course\, students are expected to exercise their own 
 professional judgement in determining how best to distribute emphasis acro
 ss the tasks\, ensuring a clear linkage between them and a clear flow of i
 deas and context.</p>\n<p>&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><strong>Assignment Tasks</strong
 ></p>\n<p><span style="text-decoration: underline\;"><strong>Task 1: Decis
 ion Context and Objective (10%)</strong></span></p>\n<p><span>In this task
 \, </span><strong>you are required to reflect on your own leadership-orien
 ted decision-making</strong><span> in a complex professional context. The 
 purpose of this task is to develop your ability to exercise </span><strong
 >judgement\, take accountability\, and demonstrate ethical awareness</stro
 ng><span>\, with AI serving as a </span><strong>supporting aid rather than
  the driver of decisions</strong><span>.</span></p>\n<p><span>You must des
 cribe a </span><strong>significant decision-making scenario from your prof
 essional experience</strong><span> (e.g.\, planning\, analysis\, prioritis
 ation\, or evaluation) and provide a </span><strong>reflective account</st
 rong><span> of how you approached\, executed\, and evaluated the decision.
  Your focus should be on </span><strong>how you thought\, reasoned\, and e
 xercised leadership</strong><span>\, not on demonstrating technical AI cap
 ability.</span></p>\n<p><span>In your response\, you should:</span></p>\n<
 ul>\n<li><span>Describe the </span><strong>decision context</strong><span>
 \, explaining why the decision was complex and required leadership judgeme
 nt.</span></li>\n<li><span>Reflect on </span><strong>how you approached th
 e decision</strong><span>\, including how you framed the problem\, evaluat
 ed options\, and managed trade-offs.</span></li>\n<li><span>Critically eva
 luate </span><strong>how the decision was ultimately made</strong><span>\,
  including what worked well and what you would do differently in hindsight
 .</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span>If you used AI tools\, you must </span><str
 ong>justify why AI support was appropriate</strong><span> in comparison to
  relying solely on your own judgement. Routine data processing or basic nu
 merical analysis alone is not considered meaningful AI use. While AI may a
 ssist with summarising information or handling large volumes of data\, thi
 s task focuses on the use of AI to </span><strong>support insight generati
 on\, structured reasoning\, and synthesis</strong><span>\, with </span><st
 rong>final accountability remaining with you as the decision-maker</strong
 ><span>.</span></p>\n<p><span>Your decision-making scenario must clearly a
 ddress the following elements:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span>Your </span><st
 rong>problem or opportunity statement</strong></li>\n<li><span>Your </span
 ><strong>decision objective(s) and success criteria</strong></li>\n<li><sp
 an>The </span><strong>scope and boundaries</strong><span> of your decision
 </span></li>\n<li><strong> </strong><span>Your </span><strong>assumptions\
 , constraints\, and key risks</strong></li>\n<li>Your leadership considera
 tions around AI use<strong>\, </strong>including where AI added value and 
 where you deliberately chose not to use it</li>\n<li>The ethical\, profess
 ional\, or organisational concerns you were mindful of throughout the deci
 sion-making process</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span>Example of Decision-making conte
 xts:</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Engineering or operations planning</li>\n<li>Su
 pply Chain\, Inventory or resource allocation decisions</li>\n<li>Project 
 management\, task prioritisation or risk assessment</li>\n<li>Performance 
 feedback or reflections on the team’s performance</li>\n<li>Strategic de
 cision-making in an organization within your work.</li>\n</ul>\n<ul>\n<li>
 Students with limited professional work experience may use relevant academ
 ic experiences\, including research projects\, internships\, or major assi
 gnments completed as part of their studies.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><span>&nbsp\;<
 /span></p>\n<p><span style="text-decoration: underline\;"><strong>Task 2: 
 PERMA-V Reflection on Decision-Making (15%)</strong></span></p>\n<p>In thi
 s task\, you are required to reflect on your decision-making experience th
 rough the PERMA-V framework\, with primary emphasis on leadership behaviou
 r\, wellbeing\, and professional judgement. The ethical and responsible us
 e of AI-based decision-support tools is permitted\, but your reflection mu
 st remain centred on human experience and accountability.</p>\n<p>You shou
 ld critically examine how the decision-making process\, including any use 
 of analytical or AI-supported insights\, influenced your own experience as
  a decision-maker and the experience of others involved.</p>\n<p>In your r
 eflection\, address the following dimensions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Posit
 ive Emotion</strong><br>How did the decision-making process affect your em
 otional state (e.g.\, confidence\, stress\, reassurance\, uncertainty)? Di
 d the availability of AI-supported insights influence emotional responses\
 , either positively or negatively?</li>\n<li><strong>Engagement</strong><b
 r>To what extent were you cognitively and emotionally engaged in the decis
 ion? Did AI-supported analysis enhance focus and sense-making\, or did it 
 risk over-reliance or reduced critical engagement?</li>\n<li><strong>Relat
 ionships</strong><br>How did the decision process affect relationships wit
 h colleagues or stakeholders? Consider whether AI-supported inputs influen
 ced trust\, credibility\, or communication dynamics.</li>\n<li><strong>Mea
 ning</strong><br>How did the decision align with your personal values and 
 leadership identity? Reflect on whether the use of AI-supported insights s
 upported or challenged your sense of purpose and responsibility.</li>\n<li
 ><strong>Accomplishment</strong><br>How do you evaluate the outcome of the
  decision in terms of effectiveness and learning? Did AI-supported reasoni
 ng contribute meaningfully to perceived success?</li>\n<li><strong>Vitalit
 y</strong><br>Reflect on the mental and emotional energy required. Did AI-
 supported assistance reduce cognitive load\, or introduce additional effor
 t through validation and oversight?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your response should d
 emonstrate reflective depth\, showing how leadership decisions (maybe supp
 orted by but not driven by AI)\, influence wellbeing and performance.</p>\
 n<p><span style="text-decoration: underline\;"><strong>Task 3: SCARF-Based
  Social and Leadership Analysis (15%)</strong></span></p>\n<p>In this task
 \, you are required to analyse the social and leadership dynamics of your 
 decision-making scenario using the SCARF framework. Ethical and transparen
 t use of AI-based decision-support tools is permitted\, and you should ref
 lect on how their use influenced social perceptions and interactions.</p>\
 n<p>You should examine how the decision process\, including the presence o
 f AI-supported recommendations or analyses\, affected the experience of th
 ose involved.</p>\n<p>Address the following dimensions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><st
 rong>Status</strong><br>How did the decision process affect perceptions of
  competence\, authority\, or expertise (yours and others’)? Did the use 
 of AI-supported insights enhance or undermine professional status?</li>\n<
 li><strong>Certainty</strong><br>Did the decision process increase clarity
  and predictability\, or introduce ambiguity? How did AI-supported analysi
 s influence your ability to manage uncertainty?</li>\n<li><strong>Autonomy
 </strong><br>How much choice and control did you and others retain? Did AI
 -supported recommendations support informed autonomy\, or constrain indepe
 ndent judgement?</li>\n<li><strong>Relatedness</strong><br>How did the dec
 ision process affect trust\, psychological safety\, and connection among s
 takeholders? Consider whether AI involvement influenced openness or scepti
 cism.</li>\n<li><strong>Fairness</strong><br>Were decisions perceived as f
 air and transparent? Reflect on whether AI-supported inputs raised or miti
 gated concerns around bias or favouritism.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Your analysis s
 hould demonstrate insight into ethical leadership and social behaviour\, r
 einforcing that AI-supported tools can influence human dynamics and must b
 e used with care and accountability.</p>\n<p><span style="text-decoration:
  underline\;"><strong>Task 4: Development and Application of a Personal Et
 hical Decision-Making Framework (60%)</strong></span></p>\n<p><span>Reflec
 t on the leadership concepts\, policies\, and decision-making approaches i
 ntroduced throughout the course and </span><strong>create a personal ethic
 al decision-making framework</strong><span>. The purpose of this task is t
 o demonstrate leadership maturity by moving beyond the application of exis
 ting models and developing a </span><strong>context-sensitive\, strengths-
 based framework</strong><span> that supports ethical and accountable judgm
 ent in complex professional situations.</span></p>\n<p><span>The proposed 
 framework should be original\, clearly structured\, and grounded in the co
 urse's learning objectives. It can focus on </span><strong>strengths profi
 le assessment</strong><span>\, including how leaders can </span><strong>ca
 pitalise on strengths while identifying and defending against weaknesses</
 strong><span> in themselves and others.</span></p>\n<p><span>The framework
  should incorporate multiple layers of influence on decision-making\, incl
 uding (but not limited to):</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span>A </span><strong>p
 yramid of influence</strong><span> covering knowledge\, skills\, behaviour
 s\, beliefs\, values\, self-image\, identity\, and purpose</span></li>\n<l
 i><span>The role of </span><strong>cognitive biases</strong><span>\, ident
 ity-based motivators\, personal characteristics\, and personality traits</
 span></li>\n<li><span>The impact of </span><strong>lifestyle and contextua
 l pressures</strong><span> on productivity\, judgement\, and behaviour</sp
 an></li>\n<li><span>The interaction between </span><strong>knowledge\, beh
 aviour\, and performance outcomes</strong></li>\n<li><span>The influence o
 f </span><strong>career and value anchors</strong><span> (e.g. Schein’s 
 anchors) on priorities\, ethical sensitivity\, and leadership judgement</s
 pan></li>\n<li><span>The developed framework should be applied to the deci
 sion context described in task 1\, but before you apply\, you’d need to 
 classify the decision context described in Task 1 before applying the fram
 ework. Clearly identify:</span></li>\n<li><span>The </span><strong>sector<
 /strong><span> in which the decision occurs (e.g. industry\, academia\, re
 search\, public sector\, healthcare\, education)</span></li>\n<li><span>Th
 e </span><strong>nature of sector-specific decision-making</strong><span> 
 involved (e.g. operational\, strategic\, ethical\, people-centred\, risk-b
 ased)</span></li>\n</ul>\n<p><span>Use this classification to justify how 
 ethical considerations\, accountability expectations\, and leadership resp
 onsibilities are framed within the scenario.</span></p>\n<ul>\n<li><span>A
 pply the proposed decision-making framework to the scenario and critically
  discuss the insights and outcomes it generates. The discussion should add
 ress the following:</span></li>\n<li><strong>Professional judgement and ac
 countability:</strong><span><br>How responsibility for decisions is retain
 ed when using decision-support tools (including analytical or AI-assisted 
 tools)\, and how the framework prevents over-reliance on such tools.</span
 ></li>\n<li><strong>Impact on people:</strong><span><br>How the framework 
 considers the effects of decisions on motivation\, trust\, autonomy\, psyc
 hological safety\, and perceptions of fairness.</span></li>\n<li><strong>B
 alancing performance\, wellbeing\, and ethics:</strong><span><br>How the f
 ramework supports ethical responsibility and wellbeing while still achievi
 ng performance outcomes in complex or uncertain situations.</span></li>\n<
 li><span>Is </span><strong>applicable to similar decisions</strong><span> 
 within the same context or sector</span></li>\n<li><span>Can be </span><st
 rong>embedded into organisational or institutional practice</strong></li>\
 n<li><span>Could function as a </span><strong>shared decision-making guide
  or “ready-reckoner”</strong><span> for </span><span>others in similar
  roles (e.g. colleagues\, peers\, or fellow students)</span></li>\n</ul>\n
 <p><span>This evaluation should reflect how individuals formalise decision
 -making practices that guide leadership judgement while remaining grounded
  in </span><strong>organisational values\, ethical principles\, and collec
 tive wisdom</strong><span>.</span></p>\n<p>Your response should demonstrat
 e thoughtful synthesis and leadership maturity\, showing how insights from
  the course help you construct a decision making model that meaningfully i
 nforms real-world leadership behaviour.</p><script src="https://instructur
