CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:The objective of this assignment is to implement an expert agen
 t to competitively battle Pokémon in Pokémon Showdown. The agent must be
  developed as an expert system - you are free to choose how you implement 
 the expert\, using any methodology or resources online that you wish. The 
 only exception is that you are NOT allowed to utilise machine learning (de
 ep learning/reinforcement learning etc) as it is not an expert system - ht
 tps://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expert_system.\n\nFull project details and imp
 lementation instructions are all found on the project Github: https://gith
 ub.com/UoA-CARES/showdown_agent#. These instructions will enable you to se
 t up the required programs to complete the assignment and provide instruct
 ions on how to submit your agent for the class competition.\n\n[in-game.pn
 g] (https://canvas.auckland.ac.nz/courses/142733/files/18251038/preview?hi
 dden=1)\n\nAssessment\n----------\n\nThis project will be assessed in two 
 pieces\, with bonus marks for the top 16 highest-performing agents in a cl
 ass-wide competition\, where agents can beat out the rest of the class.\n\
 nBot Competition Placement - 10%\n-------------------------------\n\nThe a
 gents will first be evaluated against the various generic bots designed to
  be at "easy" to "hard" difficulty levels. The marks will be determined ba
 sed on the evaluations run offline by the teaching team. Please submit you
 r best-performing bot for these marks. Full instructions on how to check a
 gainst this are given in the GitHub link above.\n\nReport Submission - 40%
 \n-----------------------\n\nYou will submit a maximum 6-page single-colum
 n (references excluded) font size 11 report on the design\, evaluation\, 
 and reflections on your chosen expert system approach. This includes all f
 igures and tables\, of which we expect to see some - we are not looking to
  read a 6-page wall of text. How you allocate the pages is up to you\, but
  we would recommend a reasonable breakdown of 2 pages of design justificat
 ions\, 2-3 pages of evaluation/discussion\, and 1-2 pages of reflections. 
 However\, it is up to you how you want to format the report overall. \n\n
 The report is evaluating your understanding of your method and practical w
 ork: we are looking for more than just "does it work". \n\nDesign\n\nWe d
 on't want a simple description of how your method works - we are looking f
 or how you designed the agent and why. We are explicitly looking to see t
 hat you understand the material we are teaching through your reasoning and
  approach to implementing your expert agent\; strong justifications and qu
 antified metrics with credible citations will demonstrate your understandi
 ng of the material. Why did you take this specific approach? is it reasona
 ble? what are the limitations of the design? how does it compare to potent
 ial alternatives? Don't just explain how the method works - but why did yo
 u take that approach in the first place? How did it\, and your understandi
 ng of its design\, evolve? \n\nIf you have multiple iterations\, you can 
 contrast and compare the designs as you developed them - comparing perform
 ance evaluations would be excellent.\n\nEvaluation\n\nCritically\, the rep
 ort will present quantified performance metrics that demonstrate how well 
 the approach performed to justify and explain the outcome of your chosen m
 ethodology. Demonstrate that your agent is operating as intended and expec
 ted\; simply placing well in the tournament is not enough to demonstrate t
 hat the method you have taken is operating as designed. It is important th
 at you present quality\, quantified performance measures for the system yo
 u have developed because these metrics provide objective evidence of how w
 ell your system meets its intended goals. Clear\, data-driven evaluation a
 llows others to understand the effectiveness\, efficiency\, and reliabilit
 y of your solution. It also helps identify strengths and areas for improve
 ment\, supports fair comparisons with alternative approaches\, and demonst
 rates that your design decisions are supported by measurable outcomes rath
 er than assumptions or anecdotal evidence. Quantified results are essentia
 l for transparency\, credibility\, and informed discussion of your work. T
 he report can speak to various iterations or expert agent designs\, with d
 iscussions comparing each driven by quantified performance evaluations. \
 n\nDon't just measure things for the sake of measuring things\, though\; c
 onsider the relevance of what you are trying to demonstrate. \n\nReflecti
 ons\n\nReflect on the expectations of your approach and how the evaluation
  of those expectations compares - what worked\, what didn't\, and can you 
 demonstrate why not? Strongly consider the requirements\, challenges\, and
  limitations of developing expert systems and consider how that relates or
  is exemplified in the given task. What did this experience teach you abou
 t the nature of expert agents? \n\nClass Competition - 5% Bonus Marks\n--
 --------------------------------\n\nThe class competition will assess the 
 quality of your expert agent against the rest of the class. This is also a
  chance to earn bonus marks by potentially placing the top 16 winners over
 all.\n\nSWISS Phase\n\nThe first phase of the tournament will be run as Sw
 iss-style rounds with winners vs winners\, losers vs losers (B05/B03 match
 es)\, eliminating half the players at each stage until there are only 16 p
 layers remaining. Simple Uber bots will be added to the tournament to prov
 ide equal numbers for an effective set of Swiss rounds - a total of 32\, 6
 4\, 128\, or 256 players.\n\nKnockout Phase\n\nThe final phase will place 
 the top 16 in head-to-head B03 matches\, seeding the top-placing teams aga
 inst the lowest-seeding teams until the grand finals.\n\nThe top placement
  will also win a neat trophy!
SEQUENCE:0
SUMMARY:Expert Systems - Pokemon Showdown (Lane 2) [COMPSYS 726]
URL;VALUE=URI:https://canvas.auckland.ac.nz/calendar?include_contexts=cours
 e_142733&month=08&year=2026#assignment_519447
X-ALT-DESC;FMTTYPE=text/html:<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://instructu
 re-uploads-apse2.s3.ap-southeast-2.amazonaws.com/account_61990000000000001
 /attachments/18034557/prod-uoa-canvas-mobile.css"><p>The objective of this
  assignment is to implement an expert agent to competitively battle Pokém
 on in Pokémon Showdown. The agent must be developed as an expert system -
  you are free to choose how you implement the expert\, using any methodolo
 gy or resources online that you wish. The only exception is that you are <
 strong>NOT</strong> allowed to utilise machine learning (deep learning/rei
 nforcement learning etc) as it is not an expert system - <a href="https://
 en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expert_system">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Expert_
 system</a>.</p>\n<p>Full project details and implementation instructions a
 re all found on the project Github: <a class="inline_disabled" href="https
 ://github.com/UoA-CARES/showdown_agent#" target="_blank">https://github.co
 m/UoA-CARES/showdown_agent#</a>. These instructions will enable you to set
  up the required programs to complete the assignment and provide instructi
 ons on how to submit your agent for the class competition.</p>\n<p style="
 text-align: center\;"><img src="https://canvas.auckland.ac.nz/courses/1427
 33/files/18251038/preview?hidden=1" alt="in-game.png" width="642" height="
 361" data-api-endpoint="https://canvas.auckland.ac.nz/api/v1/courses/14273
 3/files/18251038" data-api-returntype="File" loading="lazy"></p>\n<h3>Asse
 ssment</h3>\n<p>This project will be assessed in two pieces\, with bonus m
 arks for the top 16 highest-performing agents in a class-wide competition\
 , where agents can beat out the rest of the class.</p>\n<h4><strong>Bot Co
 mpetition Placement - 10%</strong></h4>\n<p>The agents will first be evalu
 ated against the various generic bots designed to be at "easy" to "hard" d
 ifficulty levels. The marks will be determined based on the evaluations ru
 n offline by the teaching team. Please submit your <em><span style="text-d
 ecoration: underline\;"><strong>best-performing</strong></span></em> bot f
 or these marks. Full instructions on how to check against this are given i
 n the GitHub link above.</p>\n<h4><strong>Report Submission - 40% </strong
 ></h4>\n<p>You will submit a <strong>maximum</strong> 6<strong>-page singl
 e-column (references excluded) font size 11 </strong>report on the <strong
 ><em><span style="text-decoration: underline\;">design\,</span></em></stro
 ng> <span style="text-decoration: underline\;"><strong><em>evaluation\,</e
 m></strong></span><strong>&nbsp\;</strong>and <em><span style="text-decora
 tion: underline\;"><strong>reflections</strong></span></em> on your chosen
  expert system approach. This includes all figures and tables\, of which w
 e expect to see some - we are not looking to read a 6-page wall of text. H
 ow you allocate the pages is up to you\, but we would recommend a reasonab
 le breakdown of 2 pages of design justifications\, 2-3 pages of evaluation
 /discussion\, and 1-2 pages of reflections. However\, it is up to you how 
 you want to format the report overall.&nbsp\;</p>\n<p>The report is evalua
 ting your understanding of your method and practical work: we are looking 
 for more than just <strong>"does it work"</strong>.&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><strong
 >Design</strong></p>\n<p>We don't want a simple description of how your me
 thod works - we are looking for how you designed the agent and <em><span s
 tyle="text-decoration: underline\;"><strong>why</strong></span></em>.&nbsp
 \;We are explicitly looking to see that you <em><span style="text-decorati
 on: underline\;">understand the material</span></em> we are teaching throu
 gh your reasoning and approach to implementing your expert agent\; strong 
 <em><span style="text-decoration: underline\;">justifications</span></em> 
 and <span style="text-decoration: underline\;"><em>quantified </em></span>
 metrics<em> </em><em></em>with credible citations will demonstrate your un
 derstanding of the material. Why did you take this specific approach? is i
 t reasonable? what are the limitations of the design? how does it compare 
 to potential alternatives? Don't just explain how the method works - but w
 hy did you take that approach in the first place? How did it\, and your un
 derstanding of its design\, evolve?&nbsp\;</p>\n<p>If you have multiple it
 erations\, you can contrast and compare the designs as you developed them 
 - comparing performance evaluations would be excellent.</p>\n<p><strong>Ev
 aluation</strong></p>\n<p>Critically\, the report will present <strong><em
 ><span style="text-decoration: underline\;">quantified</span></em></strong
 > performance metrics that demonstrate how well the approach performed to 
 justify and explain the outcome of your chosen methodology. Demonstrate th
 at your agent is operating as intended and expected\; simply placing well 
 in the tournament is not enough to demonstrate that the method you have ta
 ken is operating as designed. It is important that you present quality\, q
 uantified performance measures for the system you have developed because t
 hese metrics provide objective evidence of how well your system meets its 
 intended goals. Clear\, <em><span style="text-decoration: underline\;"><st
 rong>data-driven evaluation</strong></span></em> allows others to understa
 nd the effectiveness\, efficiency\, and reliability of your solution. It a
 lso helps identify strengths and areas for improvement\, supports fair com
 parisons with alternative approaches\, and demonstrates that your design d
 ecisions are supported by measurable outcomes rather than assumptions or a
 necdotal evidence. Quantified results are essential for transparency\, cre
 dibility\, and informed discussion of your work. The report can speak to v
 arious iterations or expert agent designs\, with discussions comparing eac
 h driven by quantified performance evaluations.&nbsp\;</p>\n<p>Don't just 
 measure things for the sake of measuring things\, though\; consider the re
 levance of what you are trying to demonstrate.&nbsp\;</p>\n<p><strong>Refl
 ections</strong></p>\n<p>Reflect on the expectations of your approach and 
 how the evaluation of those expectations compares - what worked\, what did
 n't\, and can you demonstrate why not? Strongly consider the requirements\
 , challenges\, and limitations of developing expert systems and consider h
 ow that relates or is exemplified in the given task. What did this experie
 nce teach you about the nature of expert agents?&nbsp\;</p>\n<h4><strong>C
 lass Competition - 5% Bonus Marks</strong></h4>\n<p>The class competition 
 will assess the quality of your expert agent against the rest of the class
 . This is also a chance to earn <em><strong>bonus</strong></em> marks by p
 otentially placing the top 16 winners overall.</p>\n<div class="markdown-h
 eading">\n<p class="heading-element"><strong>SWISS Phase</strong></p>\n</d
 iv>\n<p>The first phase of the tournament will be run as Swiss-style round
 s with winners vs winners\, losers vs losers (B05/B03 matches)\, eliminati
 ng half the players at each stage until there are only 16 players remainin
 g. Simple Uber bots will be added to the tournament to provide equal numbe
 rs for an effective set of Swiss rounds - a total of 32\, 64\, 128\, or 25
 6 players.</p>\n<div class="markdown-heading">\n<p class="heading-element"
 ><strong>Knockout Phase</strong></p>\n</div>\n<p>The final phase will plac
 e the top 16 in head-to-head B03 matches\, seeding the top-placing teams a
 gainst the lowest-seeding teams until the grand finals.</p>\n<p>The top pl
 acement will also win a neat trophy!</p><script src="https://instructure-u
 ploads-apse2.s3.ap-southeast-2.amazonaws.com/account_61990000000000001/att
 achments/18034556/prod-uoa-canvas-mobile-clear.js"></script>
END:VEVENT
