DESCRIPTION: \n\nWeighting: 30%\n\nIndividual/team: Team\n\nThe due date f
 or uploading your group video presentation is 20 September.\n\nEach team w
 ill be assigned a presentation week between Week 10 and Week 11. Your spec
 ific week will be confirmed in advance.\n\n-------------------\nAssignment
  overview\n-------------------\n\nThis assignment aims to develop your tea
 mwork\, communication\, and presentation skills. You will work in teams to
  create a 5-minute presentation on a leadership or professional skills-rel
 ated topic.\n\nThis assignment supports course learning outcomes 2\, 3\, 4
  and 5.\n\n------------------\nAssignment details\n------------------\n\nT
 ask\n----\n\nStep 1: Check your presentation date. Note the week and ensur
 e you are available during that time.\n\nNote: Only one team member needs 
 to submit this.\n\nStep 2: Submit your Video presentation to Canvas on the
  due date mentioned in the assignment\n\nStep 3: Engage in a 10-minute Q&A
  session.\n\nYour presentation will be assessed by both peers and teaching
  staff\, focusing on content quality\, clarity\, teamwork\, collaboration\
 , delivery\, and overall professionalism. Therefore\, attendance is mandat
 ory for all team presentations by at least two other teams. Feedback will 
 be provided in class.\n\nExample Topics\n--------------\n\n* Building resi
 lience through AI-assisted tools in professional environments\n\n* Network
 ing effectively in hybrid and digital-first career landscapes\n\n* Managin
 g stress\, preventing burnout\, and promoting workplace wellness\n\n* Leve
 raging feedback for continuous improvement and personal growth\n\n* Master
 ing virtual collaboration and remote team leadership\n\n* Cultivating a gr
 owth mindset amid rapid technological disruptions\n\n* Ethical decision-ma
 king in AI-driven workplaces\n\n* Adapting to emerging technologies like g
 enerative AI and automation\n\n* Mentorship dynamics: Guiding and learning
  in diverse\, multigenerational teams\n\n* Advanced time management strate
 gies for high-performance roles\n\n* Crafting a professional online presen
 ce with personal branding\n\n* Overcoming leadership challenges in entry-l
 evel and transitional jobs\n\n* Fostering a strong professional work ethic
  in uncertain times\n\n* Navigating cultural and inclusive differences for
  equitable workplaces\n\n* Delivering impactful presentations with data vi
 sualization and storytelling\n\n* Developing emotional intelligence for hu
 man-centred leadership\n\n* Championing continuous upskilling and lifelong
  learning paths\n\n* Building trust and psychological safety in teams\n\n*
  Strategic thinking for sustainable and purpose-driven leadership\n\n* Con
 flict resolution in fast-paced\, collaborative settings\n\n \n\nSupportin
 g resources\n\nThis assignment draws on the content presented in Modules 1
 –6.\n\nRefer to your [Assignment briefcase] (https://canvas.auckland.ac
 .nz/courses/145185/pages/assignment-briefcase-2-2) for general support on 
 researching\, writing and referencing academic assignments.\n\nUse the [Qu
 ickcite] (https://www.cite.auckland.ac.nz/2.html) (University of Auckland\
 , n.d.) tool for assistance with referencing.\n\nSelect each of the follow
 ing headings to learn more about the assignment criteria\, submission deta
 ils\, the use of Gen-AI and your assessment declaration.\n\n[Marking crite
 ria ] (#)\n-----------------------\n\n* Content\n\n* Delivery\n\n* Teamwor
 k\n\n* Effectively answering Q&A.\n\n \n\n[Submission details] (#)\n-----
 -------------------\n\nThis assignment will be submitted through Canvas. W
 hen you are ready to submit your assignment\, select the 'Start Assignment
 ' button on this page and then scroll down to the 'File Upload' tab\, wher
 e you can choose your file.\n\nIf your assignment has multiple files\, sel
 ect 'Add Another File' for each subsequent file before ticking the 'Agree'
  check box\, then select 'Submit Assignment'.\n\nPlease note: When you sub
 mit your assignment through Canvas\, you also submit it through Turnitin. 
 Turnitin is a text-matching service that compares your work with an intern
 ational database of information sources. You will need to agree to use it.
 \n\n[Using Gen-AI ] (#)\n-------------------\n\nGenerative Artificial Inte
 lligence\, or Gen-AI\, is a technology used to generate text\, images\, da
 ta\, and videos in response to specific prompts.\n\nCourse Coordinators wi
 ll let you know where and when it is appropriate to use ChatGPT or other G
 enerative Artificial Intelligence generation software in assignments. As G
 en-AI is not allowed in all assignments\, it is important to check the gui
 delines on the permitted use of software in assignment activities. If you 
 are unsure if you can use Gen-AI for a particular assignment\, please cont
 act your course coordinator.\n\nPlease remember your responsibility to uph
 old academic integrity. Submitting work for assessment that is not your wo
 rk is considered academic misconduct.\n\nFor more information\, please see
  Advice for Students on Using Generative Artificial Intelligence.\n\n[Assi
 gnment declaration ] (#)\n-----------------------------\n\nUoA Online – 
 Assessment Declaration and Statement of Authorship\n----------------------
 -----------------------------------------\n\nBefore submitting your assign
 ment\, please ensure you have completed and understood the University of A
 uckland [Academic Integrity Course] (https://www.auckland.ac.nz/en/student
 s/forms-policies-and-guidelines/student-policies-and-guidelines/academic-i
 ntegrity-copyright/academic-integrity-course.html).\n\nBy submitting my as
 signment\, I declare that:\n\n* I have read and understood the University'
 s [Student Academic Conduct Statute] (https://www.auckland.ac.nz/en/studen
 ts/forms-policies-and-guidelines/student-policies-and-guidelines/academic-
 integrity-copyright/university-regulations-statutes-guidelines.html)\, as 
 well as the University regulations\, statutes and guidelines on [Academic 
 Integrity] (https://www.auckland.ac.nz/en/students/forms-policies-and-guid
 elines/student-policies-and-guidelines/academic-integrity-copyright.html).
 \n\n* What I am submitting is an original piece of work\, and no part has 
 been completed by a person or persons other than myself.\n\n* No part of t
 his work has been copied or paraphrased from any other source except where
  this has been clearly and correctly acknowledged in the body of the assig
 nment and included in the reference list.\n\n* I have retained a copy of t
 his assignment in the event it becomes lost or damaged.\n\nI agree and ack
 nowledge that:\n\n* I have read and understood the declaration and stateme
 nt of authorship above.\n\n* I accept that the use of my University of Auc
 kland account to electronically submit this assignment constitutes my agre
 ement to the UoA Online Assessment Declaration and Statement of Authorship
 .\n\n* If I do not agree to the UoA Online Assessment Declaration in this 
 context\, the assignment will not be valid for assessment purposes and wil
 l not be included in the aggregate score for this course.\n\n* I am aware 
 that it is not acceptable to resubmit the same piece of work (in part or a
 s a whole) for multiple assessments without permission from the Course Coo
 rdinator.\n\nFurther information relating to the penalties for plagiarism\
 , which range from a formal instance of academic misconduct to cancellatio
 n of a student's enrolment at the University\, is contained in the Univers
 ity of Auckland [Statute for Student Discipline (2013)] (https://www.auckl
 and.ac.nz/en/students/forms-policies-and-guidelines/student-policies-and-g
 uidelines/academic-integrity-copyright/university-regulations-statutes-gui
 delines.html).
