CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:Total Word/time limit: 2000 (+/− 10%)\n\nThe KXZY Case Study 
 1000 (+/− 10%)\n\nSelf learning and reflection 1000 (+/− 10%)\n\nWeigh
 ting: 25%\n\nIndividual/team: Individual\n\nIf anything about assignment 3
  is unclear\, post your question on the[ discussion board.] (https://canv
 as.auckland.ac.nz/courses/145185/discussion_topics/1637604) You can connec
 t with fellow students there\, and a graduate teaching assistant will resp
 ond to any unanswered queries.\nFor questions that can’t be resolved on 
 the board or any other issues\, contact Salik Khan directly at [salik.kha
 n@auckland.ac.nz.] (mailto:salik.khan@auckland.ac.nz)\n\n[Assignment Overv
 iew] (#)\n-------------------------\n\nThis assignment is divided into two
  parts. \n\n* Part 1: The KXZY Case Study (15%)\n\n* Part 2: Self Learnin
 g and reflection (10%)\n\nThis assignment supports course learning outcome
 s 1\, 2\, 3\, 4 and 5.\n\nDownload the case study KXZY ==> [Case Study KXZ
 Y] (https://canvas.auckland.ac.nz/courses/145185/files/17888555?wrap=1)\n\
 n[] (https://canvas.auckland.ac.nz/courses/140141/files/17059265?wrap=1)\n
 \n[Assignment Details] (#)\n------------------------\n\nPart 1: Case Study
 : “The Talent Trap” at KXZY Systems (15 marks)\n\nRead the content men
 tioned in the PDF (step 1) attached titled “KXZY case study’ and answe
 r the following questions\, drawn from the case study:\n\nQ1. Bias Identif
 ication and Interactions (3 marks)\n\nWhat types of interpersonal and syst
 emic biases influenced the promotion decision at KXZY Systems? How did the
 se biases interact to shape the outcome?\n\nThink carefully about the indi
 vidual biases covered in your lectures (such as affinity bias\, halo effec
 t\, attribution bias\, etc.) and map each bias to specific situations or d
 ecisions described in the case. There may be multiple valid interpretation
 s\, but aim to provide well-reasoned\, evidence-based explanations. These 
 insights will be crucial for informing how you propose to reshape the prom
 otion process and overall culture.\n\nQ2. Cultural and Team Consequences (
 4  marks)\n\nWhat are the long-term risks of unchecked bias for the organ
 isation (e.g.\, revenue\, brand\, retention)?\n\nConsider workload imbalan
 ce\, disengagement\, conflict\, and how trust may have eroded across teams
 . Try to reflect on real-world case studies or organisational examples tha
 t provide structural evidence of similar issues. You may reference well-kn
 own models or examples from other companies to support your points and dem
 onstrate broader understanding.\n\nQ3. Rebuilding Trust and Driving Change
  as a New Leader (8 marks)\n\nImagine you have just been appointed as a ne
 w department leader at KXZY\, tasked with transforming the culture and reb
 uilding trust.\n\nWhat specific actions would you take\, both interpersona
 l and organisational\, to:\n\n* Restore trust within and across teams\n\n*
  Redeployment of the existing team VS changing their culture organically.\
 n\n* Consider internal hire VS external hire.\n\n* Appointing a person fro
 m another department\, such as HR/techno-management.\n\n* Appointing a ded
 icated Chief People Officer for people and culture initiatives overall (if
  so\, how will their activities help in overcoming such situations\, and w
 hat would their daily work look like?)\n\n* We need a well-thought-out out
  detailed plan\n\n* Prevent future bias in promotions.\n\n* Ensure high pe
 rformers feel seen and valued.\n\nBe creative but practical: think about l
 eadership behaviour\, communication strategy\, policy redesign\, and long-
 term culture building.\n\nTips to answer the questions provided in a separ
 ate document titled ==> [Talent Trap Case Study Tips.pdf] (https://canvas
 .auckland.ac.nz/courses/145185/files/17888557?wrap=1)\n\nPart 2: Self and 
 Learning Reflection (10 marks)\n\nReview and consider all the concepts\, t
 heories and self-assessments we have explored across the entire course. Yo
 ur self and learning reflection can take a broad (whole of course) view\, 
 or you can focus on one or more specific aspects that resonated the most w
 ith you and inspired most of your growth as a person and/or professional.\
 n\n* Make sure you incorporate SMART goals in your reflection (Specific\, 
 Measurable\, Achievable\, Relevant\, and Time-bound) \n\n* Read the infor
 mation on self-reflection in [Module 10: Self-reflection.] (https://canva
 s.auckland.ac.nz/courses/145185/modules/420724)\n\nThis is a personal refl
 ection. You have been introduced to concepts and theories about your own f
 unctioning and flourishing\, and that of professional and leadership devel
 opment. The intent of this self and learning reflection is to reflect beyo
 nd your practical and theoretical learnings and 'stand on the balcony' of 
 self.\nUse of your entire strengths profile report is encouraged. Includin
 g the report as an appendix to your submission is optional and will not 
 form part of the word count for this assignment.\nUse the following prompt
 s to guide your reflection:\n\n* How has your sense-making been influenced
  by the information and new knowledge you are gathering in this course?\n\
 n* How can you view or explain past events\, experiences\, challenges\, a
 nd adversity in different ways by looking through the lens of new concepts
  you have been exposed to during this course?\n\n* How is this course infl
 uencing\, broadening or shaping your perspectives on the concept of a high
 ly effective and flourishing person and professional?\n\n* What core belie
 f(s) did you hold coming into the course\, and how have these beliefs (s) 
 shifted?\n\n* What key learning are you taking away\, and how do you inten
 d to adopt and integrate these in your personal and professional life post
  this course?\n\nSupporting Resources\n\n* This assignment draws on the co
 ntent presented in Modules 1–10.\n\n* Refer to your [Assignment briefcas
 e] (https://canvas.auckland.ac.nz/courses/145185/pages/assignment-briefcas
 e-2-2) for general support on researching\, writing and referencing academ
 ic assignments.\n\n* Use the [Quickcite] (https://www.cite.auckland.ac.nz/
 2.html) (University of Auckland\, n.d.) tool for assistance with referenci
 ng.\n\n* To assist you with completing this assignment\, you may wish to r
 efer to [Reflective writing] (https://www.learninghub.ac.nz/writing/reflec
 tive-writing/) (University of Auckland\, n.d.) in the University of Auckla
 nd Learning Hub.\n\n[Assessment Criteria] (#)\n-------------------------\n
 \nSee Rubric Below.\n\n[Submission Details] (#)\n------------------------\
 n\nThis assignment will be submitted through Canvas. When you are ready to
  submit your assignment\, select the 'Start Assignment' button on this pag
 e and then scroll down to the 'File Upload' tab\, where you can choose you
 r file.\n\nIf your assignment has multiple files\, select 'Add Another Fil
 e' for each subsequent file before ticking the 'Agree' check box\, then se
 lect 'Submit Assignment'.\n\nPlease note: When you submit your assignment 
 through Canvas\, you are also submitting the assignment through Turnitin. 
 Turnitin is a text-matching service that compares your work with an intern
 ational database of information sources. You will need to agree to use it\
 n\nUsing Gen AI\n\nGenerative Artificial Intelligence\, or Gen-AI\, is a t
 echnology that is used to generate text\, images\, data\, and videos in re
 sponse to specific prompts.\n\nCourse Coordinators will let you know where
  and when it is appropriate to use Chatgpt or other Generative Artificial 
 Intelligence software in assessments. As Gen-AI is not allowed in all asse
 ssments\, it is important to check the guidelines on the permitted use of 
 software in assessment activities. If you are unsure if you can use Gen-AI
  in a particular assessment\, please contact your Course Coordinator\n\nPl
 ease remember your responsibility to uphold academic integrity. Submitting
  work for assessment that is not your own work is considered academic misc
 onduct.\n\nFor more information\, please see [Advice for students on using
  Generative Artificial Intelligence] (https://www.auckland.ac.nz/en/studen
 ts/forms-policies-and-guidelines/student-policies-and-guidelines/academic-
 integrity-copyright/advice-for-student-on-using-generative-ai.html).\n\n[A
 ssignment Declaration ] (#)\n-----------------------------\n\nUoA Online 
 – Assessment Declaration and Statement of Authorship\n------------------
 ---------------------------------------------\n\nBefore submitting your as
 signment please ensure you have completed and understood the University of
  Auckland [Academic Integrity Course] (https://www.auckland.ac.nz/en/stude
 nts/forms-policies-and-guidelines/student-policies-and-guidelines/academic
 -integrity-copyright/academic-integrity-course.html).\n\nBy submitting my 
 assignment\, I declare that:\n\n* I have read and understood the Universit
 y's [Student Academic Conduct Statute] (https://www.auckland.ac.nz/en/stud
 ents/forms-policies-and-guidelines/student-policies-and-guidelines/academi
 c-integrity-copyright/university-regulations-statutes-guidelines.html)\, a
 s well as the University regulations\, statutes and guidelines on [Academi
 c Integrity] (https://www.auckland.ac.nz/en/students/forms-policies-and-gu
 idelines/student-policies-and-guidelines/academic-integrity-copyright.html
 ).\n\n* What I am submitting is an original piece of work\, and no part ha
 s been completed by a person or persons other than myself.\n\n* No part of
  this work has been copied or paraphrased from any other source except whe
 re this has been clearly and correctly acknowledged in the body of the ass
 ignment and included in the reference list.\n\n* I have retained a copy of
  this assignment in the event of it becoming lost or damaged.\n\nI agree a
 nd acknowledge that:\n\n* I have read and understood the declaration and s
 tatement of authorship above.\n\n* I accept that the use of my University 
 of Auckland account to electronically submit this assignment constitutes m
 y agreement to the UoA Online Assessment Declaration and Statement of Auth
 orship.\n\n* If I do not agree to the UoA Online Assessment Declaration in
  this context\, the assignment will not be valid for assessment purposes a
 nd will not be included in the aggregate score for this course.\n\n* I am 
 aware that it is not acceptable to resubmit the same piece of work (in par
 t or as a whole) for multiple assessments without permission from the Cour
 se Coordinator.\n\nFurther information relating to the penalties for plagi
 arism\, which range from a formal instance of academic misconduct to cance
 llation of a student's enrolment at the University\, is contained in the U
 niversity of Auckland [Statute for Student Discipline (2013)] (https://www
 .auckland.ac.nz/en/students/forms-policies-and-guidelines/student-policies
 -and-guidelines/academic-integrity-copyright/university-regulations-statut
 es-guidelines.html).
