CLASS:PUBLIC
DESCRIPTION:The objective of this assignment is to implement your version o
 f the "showdown_environment.py" environment to enable a Reinforcement Lear
 ning agent to learn to competitively battle Pokémon in the Pokémon Showd
 own game. You are free to choose how you implement the environment\, using
  any methodology or resources online that you wish.\n\nThe core objective 
 is to consider what information is required for the agent and to get hands
 -on experience using machine learning to solve a complex task. Your explor
 ation into solving this problem will provide key insights into the materia
 l\, which you will demonstrate in the report by explaining how you approac
 hed solving this complex task with reinforcement learning and what you exp
 erienced along the way. This is not a trivial task to learn - demonstratin
 g that you can connect the dots in the lectures to the challenges you face
  throughout the assignment is what we are looking for.\n\nFull project det
 ails and implementation instructions are all found on the project GitHub: 
 https://github.com/UoA-CARES/showdown_gym. These instructions will enable 
 you to set up the required programs to complete the assignment and provide
  instructions on how to submit your agent for the class competition.\n\n[i
 n-game-1.png] (https://canvas.auckland.ac.nz/courses/142733/files/18251037
 /preview?hidden=1)\n\n \n\nAssessment\n----------\n\nThis project will be
  assessed in two pieces.\n\nMax Expert Agent - 10%\n----------------------
 \n\nYour trained agent will be evaluated based on its performance against 
 the max expert agent over 100 battles through testing commands in the gym 
 environment. You can run this locally using the instructions on GitHub to 
 get an indication of your mark and metrics for your report. The final mark
  used for your grade will be scored by running the code locally by the tea
 ching staff.\n\nReport Submission - 40%\n-----------------------\n\nYou wi
 ll submit a maximum 6-page single-column (references excluded) font size 1
 1 report on the design\, evaluation\, and reflections on your chosen expe
 rt system approach. This includes all figures and tables\, of which we exp
 ect to see some - we are not looking to read a 6-page wall of text. How yo
 u allocate the pages is up to you\, but we would recommend a reasonable br
 eakdown of 2 pages of design justifications\, 2-3 pages of evaluation/disc
 ussion\, and 1-2 pages of reflections. However\, it is up to you how you w
 ant to format the report overall. \n\nThe report is evaluating your under
 standing of your method and practical work: we are looking for more than j
 ust "does it work". \n\nDesign\n\nWe don't want a simple description of h
 ow your method works - we are looking for how you designed the agent and w
 hy. We are explicitly looking to see that you understand the material we 
 are teaching through your reasoning and approach to implementing your expe
 rt agent\; strong justifications and quantified metrics with credible cita
 tions will demonstrate your understanding of the material. Why did you tak
 e this specific approach? is it reasonable? what are the limitations of th
 e design? how does it compare to potential alternatives? Don't just explai
 n how the method works - but why did you take that approach in the first p
 lace? How did it\, and your understanding of its design\, evolve? \n\nIf 
 you have multiple iterations\, you can contrast and compare the designs as
  you developed them - comparing performance evaluations would be excellent
 .\n\nEvaluation\n\nCritically\, the report will present quantified perform
 ance metrics that demonstrate how well the approach performed to justify a
 nd explain the outcome of your chosen methodology. Demonstrate that your a
 gent is operating as intended and expected\; simply placing well in the to
 urnament is not enough to demonstrate that the method you have taken is op
 erating as designed. It is important that you present quality\, quantified
  performance measures for the system you have developed because these metr
 ics provide objective evidence of how well your system meets its intended 
 goals. Clear\, data-driven evaluation allows others to understand the effe
 ctiveness\, efficiency\, and reliability of your solution. It also helps i
 dentify strengths and areas for improvement\, supports fair comparisons wi
 th alternative approaches\, and demonstrates that your design decisions ar
 e supported by measurable outcomes rather than assumptions or anecdotal ev
 idence. Quantified results are essential for transparency\, credibility\, 
 and informed discussion of your work. The report can speak to various iter
 ations or expert agent designs\, with discussions comparing each driven by
  quantified performance evaluations. \n\nDon't just measure things for th
 e sake of measuring things\, though\; consider the relevance of what you a
 re trying to demonstrate. \n\nReflections\n\nReflect on the expectations 
 of your approach and how the evaluation of those expectations compares - w
 hat worked\, what didn't\, and can you demonstrate why not? Strongly consi
 der the requirements\, challenges\, and limitations of reinforcement learn
 ing systems and consider how that relates or is exemplified in the given t
 ask. What did this experience teach you about the nature of reinforcement 
 learning? \n\n 
